The error clearly indicates that the algorithm produced an image with zero dimensions, causing the OpenCV display function to fail.

\section{Conclusion and Future Work}

The theoretical extension from single-factor to dual-factor scaling introduces significant complexity in coordinate mapping and pixel computation. While the algorithmic design addresses the four operational modes, the implementation suffers from fundamental issues in dimension calculation and memory access.

\subsection{Immediate Corrections Needed}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dimension Verification}: Implement robust dimension calculation with proper integer rounding and boundary checks.
    \item \textbf{Coordinate Validation}: Add bounds checking in the coordinate mapping functions to prevent out-of-range accesses.
    \item \textbf{Debugging Infrastructure}: Incorporate intermediate result visualization to identify where the dimension calculation fails.
    \item \textbf{Unit Testing}: Develop test cases for each of the four scaling scenarios with known input-output pairs.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Future Directions}

Future work will focus on implementing the corrections mentioned above and extending the algorithm to support more sophisticated interpolation methods (bilinear, bicubic) for the upscaling cases, which would improve image quality in asymmetric scaling scenarios.

The complexity of handling independent scaling factors underscores the importance of careful coordinate system design and thorough validation of dimension calculations in image processing algorithms.

